From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Noxwell <beebono@yahoo.com>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 20:56:33 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 201/225] arm64: dts: ums512: reserve stock widevine region at
 0xe3800000

The stock firmware reserves a 200 MB secure/DRM pool at 0xe3800000, but
declares it in a devicetree *overlay* (fragment@12) rather than the base
dts. Porting reserved-memory from the stock base dts alone therefore
misses it entirely, and comparing the two shows nothing wrong.

The secure world writes that region whether or not the kernel knows
about it. Left unreserved, the page allocator hands those pages to
userspace and they are zeroed underneath their owner. This presented as
memory corruption once an allocation grew past ~1.5 GiB and started
reaching 0xe3800000 -- so emulators tripped it while lighter workloads
never allocated enough to notice.

Confirmed by resolving the corrupted words to physical frames via
/proc/self/pagemap: 100% of bad words read back exactly zero, and the
bad pages formed one dense block at 0xe3800000..0xe97ff000, matching the
overlay node's base exactly. With the reservation in place, a 2300 MB
mlocked test buffer verifies clean where 1600 MB previously failed.

The same overlay also declares faceid-mem@fd270000 and vdsp-mem@fd770000.
Those stay unreserved for now as the camera/AI stacks are not enabled and
nothing drives that hardware; they will need reserving if that changes.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/sprd/ums512-rg-rotate.dts | 16 ++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sprd/ums512-rg-rotate.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sprd/ums512-rg-rotate.dts
index 2b04e1c0d..7aa240e57 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sprd/ums512-rg-rotate.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sprd/ums512-rg-rotate.dts
@@ -63,6 +63,22 @@ framebuffer_region: framebuffer-region@9e000000 {
             no-map;
         };
 
+		/*
+		 * Secure/DRM pool. Stock declares this in a devicetree overlay
+		 * (fragment@12) rather than the base dts, so it is easy to miss
+		 * when porting: see docs/decomp_overlay.dts.
+		 *
+		 * The secure world writes this region whether or not the kernel
+		 * knows about it. Leaving it unreserved lets the page allocator
+		 * hand it to userspace, and the pages are then zeroed underneath
+		 * their owner -- which looks exactly like memory corruption once
+		 * an allocation grows past ~1.5G and starts reaching 0xe3800000.
+		 */
+		widevine-mem@e3800000 {
+			reg = <0 0xe3800000 0 0xc800000>;
+			no-map;
+		};
+
 		linux,cma {
 			compatible = "shared-dma-pool";
 			size = <0x0 0x8000000>;
